WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:08.800
We are surrounded by air from all the directions.

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:13.980
This envelope of air around us is what we call atmosphere.

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:18.020
Does air also exert pressure on objects on the Earth?

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:19.720
In our previous video,

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:23.780
we studied that the liquid exerts pressure in all directions.

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:27.260
For example, here we have the water in a beaker.

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:31.620
Due to gravity this water is pulled down towards the Earth.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.740
If we divide this water into different layers,

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:38.400
then each layer is being pulled down by the Earth.

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:41.620
Due to this pull water exerts downward pressure

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:45.280
and this pressure is uneven throughout.

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:50.800
That is, the bottom layer experiences more pressure than the top layer.

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:55.320
This is because the weight of water above the bottom layer is more.

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:58.820
And the pressure decreases as we go upwards.

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:02.940
In fact we saw that the water exerts pressure in all directions

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:06.240
and not only in the downward direction.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:08.980
One more thing that we studied about the liquid pressure,

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:11.160
is that at any point in a liquid

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:14.540
the pressure is the same in all directions.

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:17.980
That is if I take any point here in this water,

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:23.500
the pressure exerted by water here is the same in all the directions.

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:26.240
Air also behaves in the same way.

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:27.580
Just like water

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:31.520
air is also being pulled down by the Earth's gravitational force.

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:35.960
This force applies pressure on the air in the downward direction.

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:39.200
The pressure exerted by air is called air pressure

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:41.680
or atmospheric pressure.

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:46.360
Did you know that the atmosphere is divided into different layers?

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:49.440
The atmosphere is divided into five layers

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:52.360
namely the troposphere, stratosphere,

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:53.720
mesosphere,

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:56.740
thermosphere and exosphere.

00:01:56.940 --> 00:02:00.380
The pressure in the troposphere which is the bottom most layer

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:04.100
is higher than the pressure in the layers above it.

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:07.080
And I am sure you can guess the reason why?

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:10.200
This is because the weight of the air above the troposphere

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:12.380
is greater than the weight of the air above,

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:15.760
say the stratosphere.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:19.400
The air pressure also acts in every direction,

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:22.080
and not only in the downward direction.

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:24.920
And finally just like the pressure in liquids,

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:28.440
the air pressure at a particular height above the Earth's surface,

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:31.180
is same in all directions.

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:34.000
That is, if I take these two points at the same distance

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:35.980
above the Earth's surface,

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:39.900
then the air at this point will exert an equal pressure

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:44.300
in all the directions just like the air at this point.

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:47.300
Let's look at an interesting graph now.

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:51.360
A graph which represents pressure at a particular altitude.

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:56.320
The graph also represents how the pressure varies from layer to layer.

00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:59.880
The troposphere layer is closest to the surface of the earth

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:04.480
and the pressure in this layer goes up to 100,000 Pascals.

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:10.140
Where Pascals represented by 'Pa' is the 'Si' unit of pressure.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:12.600
This is massive pressure.

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:17.240
This pressure pushes and pulls the object on the surface on the earth.

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:18.920
For example,

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:23.200
if I take a table with the upper surface of one by one meter.

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:26.500
The pressure exerted by the air on the table

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:30.760
in the downward direction is greater than one lakh Pascals.

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:34.960
This is equivalent to 10 cars being put on this table.

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:37.460
Yes, 10 cars!

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:40.200
I know what you must be thinking?

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:44.140
Why doesn't this surface collapse under this huge pressure?

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:48.780
How does it handle such massive pressure acting down on it?

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:52.360
A little while ago we said that the pressure exerted at points

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:56.600
above the Earth's surface is equal in all directions.

00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:03.040
So the pressure at every point on the table is the same in all the directions.

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:05.060
That is, if downward pressure

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:07.740
is pushing the table surface with a great force,

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:11.820
then the upward pressure is also pushing it in the upward direction

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:14.200
with the same force.

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:18.740
Both the forces are balanced and the table does not collapse.

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:23.400
As I said pressure is not only acting in these two directions.

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:27.480
It is acting on the surface in all the directions.

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:32.920
Pressures acting in opposite directions, cancel each other out.

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:37.300
Here is one cool experiment that you can also perform at home.

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:40.520
Take a plastic bottle filled with water.

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:45.860
Take a cardboard paper and place it on the open end of the bottle like this.

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:47.900
Hold this paper with your hand

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:52.320
so that it does not move from its position and invert the bottle.

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:54.460
Now let go off the paper.

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:59.140
What did you see? The cardboard paper sticks to the surface.

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:01.540
Why is it held to the surface?

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:04.660
It is held to the surface because of the air pressure

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:07.760
that is pushing the paper in the upward direction.

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:10.480
The upward air pressure is sufficient

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:14.380
to counter the downward push exerted by the water.

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:19.280
Now make few holes in the bottle at its base. What happens now?

00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:21.440
Why did the paper fall off?

00:05:21.740 --> 00:05:26.160
Did the upward pressure on it stop acting or did it reduce in the value?

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:30.800
No, it didn't. The upward pressure on it by the air remains the same.

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:34.440
But the downward pressure increased in its value.

00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:36.560
When we made holes in the bottle

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:39.780
we allowed air to flow inside it from the top.

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:43.860
The air from the top started exerting pressure on the water.

00:05:44.140 --> 00:05:47.220
The combined pressure of water and air from top

00:05:47.320 --> 00:05:51.900
could easily overcome the pressure exerted by air from the bottom.

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:57.240
Hence, there is net force acting on the paper in the downward direction.

00:05:57.300 --> 00:06:01.140
That is, there is more pressure in the downward direction.

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:06.220
That is the reason why this paper gets detached from the surface.

